<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Gaugamela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/331-bce">331 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Gaugamela · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-alexander-the-great">Wars of Alexander the Great</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Gaugamela was fought in 331 BCE in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and the Achaemenid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a>. Despite being outnumbered, Alexander won a decisive victory and Darius fled. The battle led to the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and its conquest by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela.jpg" alt="Battle of Gaugamela" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pietro da Cortona · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietro_da_Cortona_-_Battle_of_Alexander_versus_Darius_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> had lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-issus">Battle of Issus</a> to Alexander in November 333 BCE, and his wife, mother and two daughters were captured. Alexander then took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> after the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, whose satrap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mazaces">Mazaces</a> surrendered peacefully. Ancient historians report that Darius tried diplomacy three times, finally offering land west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, joint rule, a daughter in marriage and 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. The accounts differ in detail. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> has Parmenion say he would accept, and Alexander reply that so would he, if he were Parmenion. Alexander refused, insisting there could be only one king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><p>In the summer of 331 BCE Alexander crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and followed a northern route, easier for foraging and cooler. He crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> with great difficulty, and captured scouts told him that Darius waited at Gaugamela, where the ground was being levelled. Calculations put the battle on 1 October 331 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Modern scholars reject the very high ancient figures for Darius's army and accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s for Alexander's: 7,000 cavalry and 40,000 infantry. Darius cleared the ground for scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and placed himself at the centre with his best infantry, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> commanding the left flank and Mazaeus the right. Alexander took the right wing himself with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> and left Parmenion to hold the left.</p><p>Alexander led the Companions toward the right, drawing the Persian cavalry to the flanks and forcing Darius to attack. A long cavalry battle followed, in which the outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a> held on by careful use of reserves. The scythed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> were intercepted by javelin-throwers, and those that got through were let pass between gaps in the ranks. Alexander then formed a wedge and struck the weakened centre, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> says Darius was the first to turn and flee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Alexander went first to help Parmenion, whose wing was encircled, and the fiercest fighting of the day followed against the Persian right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> was surrendered by Bagophanes, and 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> were captured. Darius escaped east with a small force, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessus">Bessus</a> murdered him. Alexander gave him a full burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> and executed Bessus the next year. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> is traditionally considered to have ended with Darius's death. The site of the battlefield is uncertain, and the most commonly accepted location is Karamlesh in the Nineveh Plains.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Gaugamela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive military collapse came only with Alexander the Great's campaigns (334–330 BCE), when a tactically superior Macedonian army defeated Persian forces at the Granicus, Issus and <b>Gaugamela</b> in rapid succession.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Bactrian cavalry had long been famous: 9,000 Bactrian horsemen fought for Darius at the <b>Battle of Gaugamela</b>, and Alexander added Bactrians to his companion cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…became the satrapy of Armenia, held for three centuries by the Orontid dynasty, who after Alexander's victory at <b>Gaugamela</b> in 331 BCE and the brief tenure of the Macedonian general Neoptolemus returned not as satraps but as kings: Orontes III and Mithridates of Lesser Armenia declared themselves independent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and <b>Gaugamela</b> (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of Porus at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), successes in pitched battle and in siege warfare alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus records that Persians and Saka broke the Athenian centre at the Battle of Marathon in 490 BCE and that Saka horsemen fought at Plataea in 479 BCE, and Saka cavalry served Darius III at the <b>Battle of Gaugamela</b> in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Odrysians were long allies of Athens and joined the Peloponnesian War on its side; in the 4th century BCE Philip II of Macedon conquered southern Thrace, and Thracian cavalry and javelin men later served Alexander the Great at Issus and <b>Gaugamela</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
